= Satisficing =

== Overview ==
'''Satisficing''' is a decision strategy that searches for an option meeting an aspiration or acceptability threshold and stops when such an option is found.

== Origin ==
Herbert A. Simon developed satisficing within [[Bounded rationality]]. It contrasts with procedures that require identifying a globally optimal alternative.

== Search and stopping ==
Satisficing couples a search process to a stopping rule. Its quality depends on the aspiration level, search order, information costs, and environment.

== Not mere laziness ==
Stopping before exhaustive optimization can be adaptive when additional search is costly. A satisficing procedure can nevertheless perform poorly when thresholds or search processes are badly matched to the task.

== References ==
* Herbert A. Simon, “A Behavioral Model of Rational Choice” (1955).
* Herbert A. Simon, ''Models of Man'' (1957).
* bounded-rationality literature.

